# draft_attack_id|draft_defense_id|decision|role|applicability|limitations
1|1|CORRECT|DIRECT|Interpret source verification here as source-network IP anti-spoofing to reject forged reflection requests.|Checking an email sender does not stop reflection; victim-side filtering alone cannot control other originating networks.
2|2|RETAIN|DIRECT|Proper authenticated encryption rejects modified ciphertext before vulnerable application decryption paths expose padding information.|Correct nonce handling and implementation matter; do not claim that arbitrary encryption with an unrelated checksum suffices.
3|3|CORRECT|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|A controlled change process supports replacement of a cryptographically broken scheme after review and testing.|Change management is not chosen-plaintext security. The mapping needs explicit migration context rather than a prevention claim.
4|4|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Reduced Bluetooth discovery can lower casual exposure while approved connections remain necessary.|Known device addresses and exploitable services can remain reachable; patching or radio shutdown addresses different risks.
5|5|RETAIN|DIRECT|Disabling an unused Bluetooth interface removes that device's unsolicited Bluetooth-message delivery path.|It does not remove existing malware or protect other wireless interfaces.
6|6|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Verified dedicated physical resources can reduce a co-tenant's access to the shared resource causing a leak.|Provider terminology differs; dedicated virtual placement may still share hardware, and same-host leakage can remain.
7|7|RETAIN|DIRECT|Removing WEP and deploying a supported modern Wi-Fi configuration eliminates reliance on WEP's broken design.|Client compatibility, configuration and implementation security still need assessment; jamming is unaffected.
8|8|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Hypervisor patching, minimized virtual devices and restricted management reduce known guest-escape attack paths.|Hardening cannot prove the absence of unknown host or firmware vulnerabilities.
9|9|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Appropriate standardized post-quantum public-key mechanisms address identified quantum-vulnerable public-key operations.|Do not imply current quantum computers break every cipher, or that algorithm migration alone secures the whole protocol.
10|10|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A trusted authenticated support channel can reduce exposure of sensitive information during verified assistance.|An encrypted call can still be fraudulent; transport security alone does not defeat a convincing voice pretext.
10|11|RETAIN|DIRECT|Independent callback verification interrupts reliance on a spoofed caller identity before credentials or authority are released.|A phone number supplied by the suspect caller is not independent verification.
11|12|QUALIFY|DIRECT|An approved challenge-response identity process can test a pretexting requester's claimed identity.|Public biographical questions are weak, and proof of identity does not authorize every requested disclosure.
11|13|CORRECT|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Enforced handling procedures can define which information staff may disclose to a verified requester.|A privacy notice alone does not authenticate anyone or technically prevent pretexting.
12|14|RETAIN|DIRECT|Appropriate paper destruction removes readable sensitive records before they enter an uncontrolled waste stream.|Storage media require technology-appropriate sanitization; careless custody can expose records before destruction.
12|15|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Locked collection containers reduce access before controlled removal and destruction.|They leave the contents recoverable if the lock or collection chain is bypassed.
13|16|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A screen privacy filter reduces off-axis observation of displayed information.|It does not stop a camera directly behind the user or observation of keyboard input.
13|17|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A suitable biometric sign-in can reduce typing of reusable secrets that an observer could record.|It does not conceal an unlocked screen, prevent screen capture or solve every authentication-recovery path.
14|18|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Restricting application permissions can reduce accessible sensors and personal data before a low-privilege spyware program collects them.|Privileged malware may bypass settings; permission review does not reliably remove an active infection.
14|19|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Anti-spyware detection and remediation can identify and remove supported surveillance software.|Novel or privileged surveillance may evade detection, and prior data theft cannot be undone by removal.
15|20|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Time-limited codes narrow the useful lifetime of captured authentication values when the verifier enforces appropriate checks.|Codes can be relayed or reused inside a window without single-use enforcement; they do not protect arbitrary messages.
15|21|RETAIN|DIRECT|An authenticated fresh challenge or one-time nonce can bind a credential proof to its intended exchange.|The receiver must verify freshness or non-reuse; simply including randomness is insufficient.
16|22|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Verified distribution sources reduce deceptive or bundled software acquisition.|Legitimate publishers can distribute unwanted software or suffer compromise; reputation is not an integrity guarantee.
16|23|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Browser ad-request filtering reduces unwanted advertisements and some malvertising exposure.|It does not uninstall all adware or establish that all permitted content is safe.
17|24|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Protected integrity baselines can reveal altered boot or system components associated with a rootkit.|A compromised kernel can deceive in-guest tools; baseline and measurement trust must be independent where needed.
17|25|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Verified boot-chain policy can reject unauthorized startup components and reduce some bootkit persistence.|Signed vulnerable components, compromised firmware and post-boot rootkits remain possible.
18|26|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Process memory inspection can find injected or memory-resident code not represented by a malicious executable file.|Visibility and rapid execution limit coverage; fileless activity can still leave other artifacts.
18|27|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Behavior analytics can identify suspicious use of interpreters and legitimate utilities in a fileless execution chain.|Legitimate automation can look unusual; alerts need context and response rather than automatic certainty.
19|28|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Compatible frequency-agile radios may tolerate some narrow-band interference by using other permitted channels.|Wideband or powerful jamming can affect all channels; this is not universal anti-jamming protection.
19|29|REMOVE_PROPOSED|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Backups may support recovery if a disruption coincides with data loss.|They do not restore radio availability or counter interference; do not teach backups as a jamming defense.
20|30|RETAIN|DETECTION|Calibrated sensors provide early measurements of heat, water, smoke or power conditions threatening equipment.|Sensors do not repair the condition or determine whether the cause is deliberate.
20|31|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Independent power, cooling or service paths can preserve operation when an environmental component fails.|Shared failure domains and untested failover can defeat redundancy; backups and redundancy are different controls.
21|32|CORRECT|DIRECT|Use a cost-tuned salted password KDF to increase the work required per offline password guess.|A fast general-purpose hash, even a modern one, is not adequate password storage merely because it is called strong.
22|33|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Configuration and access audits can discover public permissions and verify their remediation.|An audit does not itself remove an exposed bucket policy and can miss changes between checks.
22|34|CORRECT|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Encryption protects some storage or transport exposure under appropriate key and access arrangements.|It does not fix anonymous authorized reads when the service decrypts objects for public users; remove public permissions instead.
23|35|RETAIN|DIRECT|Supported removal tools uninstall unneeded packages and associated services after dependency assessment.|Not all bloatware is malware; privileged compromise may require a more trustworthy recovery process.
23|36|QUALIFY|DIRECT|A verified clean image can remove accumulated unwanted software when rebuilding is appropriate.|Rebuilding is disruptive and may reinstall the same bundled programs if the image is not corrected.
24|37|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Monitoring domains, ads and abuse reports can discover impersonating content and support response.|Detection is not authentication of every message and does not automatically remove a fraudulent site.
24|38|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Education helps users check trusted routes rather than trusting logos or familiar branding.|People cannot reliably identify every impersonation; usable verification and technical controls remain necessary.
25|39|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Schema validation rejects malformed authentication messages before protocol processing.|Well-formed forged assertions remain possible without signature, issuer and freshness checks.
25|40|RETAIN|DIRECT|Protocol-appropriate identity, signature, audience and freshness checks reject fabricated authentication assertions.|The source attack label is broad; a scenario must name the actual assertion and missing verification.
26|41|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Independent verification of a support or reward offer checks authority before the requested disclosure or installation.|A vague instruction to verify is insufficient without a trusted evidence route and authorization check.
26|42|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Practice recognizing exchanges of benefits for secrets can improve refusal and reporting decisions.|Training does not prove the actual requester is legitimate or prevent malware execution by itself.
27|37|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Physical-access monitoring can reveal unexpected accompaniment or movement after entry.|It does not necessarily stop an unauthorized visitor at the door; observation needs a safe response process.
27|43|RETAIN|DIRECT|Visitor escort procedures limit movement to approved routes and responsible accompaniment.|Compliance, staffing and safe challenge practices determine effectiveness; an unattended visitor can still cross interior boundaries.
28|44|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A correctly configured modern Wi-Fi deployment removes weaknesses associated with obsolete protocol modes.|WPA3 does not repair every access-point implementation flaw or make transition configurations uniformly safe.
28|45|RETAIN|DIRECT|Applicable verified firmware fixes address known wireless implementation vulnerabilities.|Patches must match affected devices, and compromise before patching may require additional response.
29|46|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Enforced quotas before expensive work reduce request-exhaustion paths within the service's control.|Distributed sources and constrained upstream links can defeat local request-based protection.
29|47|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Additional independently provisioned service instances can tolerate some workload pressure or instance failures.|Shared dependencies and total capacity remain finite; balancing is not a complete DoS prevention mechanism.
30|34|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Authenticated encryption protects data across a path where an intermediary could observe or alter it.|Unauthenticated encryption does not establish peer identity, and compromised endpoints remain outside that guarantee.
30|48|RETAIN|DIRECT|Mutual authentication checks both endpoint identities to resist active peer impersonation.|Trust configuration, identity binding and key protection are essential; a protocol label is not proof of correct validation.
31|49|QUALIFY|DIRECT|An appropriately chosen SHA-3 variant supplies modern collision resistance for suitable designs.|Output size and intended use matter; it is not a generic password-storage or message-authentication replacement.
31|50|RETAIN|DIRECT|Sufficient output size raises generic birthday collision-search cost for an otherwise sound hash construction.|A longer output cannot rescue an algorithm with a practical structural collision weakness.
32|51|RETAIN|DIRECT|Source-network anti-spoofing rejects forged requests before they enlist reflection amplifiers.|Deployment elsewhere is required to stop abuse from other networks; not every DDoS uses spoofed sources.
32|52|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Relevant boundary rules can close exposed amplifiers or block identifiable abusive service traffic.|The rule must be on an effective path; victim-side filtering cannot restore bandwidth already saturated upstream.
33|53|RETAIN|DIRECT|Switch ARP validation checks advertisements against trusted local address bindings.|Trust-port errors and unhandled static hosts can bypass protection or interrupt service.
33|54|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Managed fixed ARP bindings prevent unwanted dynamic replacement for selected stable peers.|Manual drift and compromised legitimate devices remain concerns; this does not scale automatically.
34|55|RETAIN|DIRECT|Correct DNSSEC validation authenticates signed DNS data against the configured chain of trust.|Unsigned data, registrar compromise and traffic confidentiality are different problems.
34|56|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Hardened resolvers with appropriate validation and controlled access reduce cache-poisoning exposure.|A resolver's reputation alone does not establish that every response is authenticated.
35|57|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Correct context-specific escaping can reduce unsafe shell interpretation in constrained legacy paths.|Prefer avoiding a shell; quoting errors and argument injection can remain even when some metacharacters are escaped.
35|58|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Explicit allowlists constrain permitted operations and inputs when combined with safe non-shell interfaces.|An approved string can still be interpreted unsafely downstream; verify the complete command construction.
36|59|RETAIN|DIRECT|Reviewed constant-time sensitive operations reduce certain secret-dependent timing and cache leaks.|The deployed compiler and platform matter; power, fault and other side channels need separate treatment.
36|60|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|An assessed HSM can confine keys and sensitive operations within a hardened boundary.|Not every HSM or operation is free of side channels, and authorized misuse of the key can still occur.
37|61|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Independent source review can identify suspicious destructive triggers before acceptance.|Review may miss obfuscation, dependencies or code introduced after review in the build process.
37|62|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Restricting changes to sensitive code and scheduled jobs reduces opportunities to plant destructive triggers.|An already authorized maintainer may still abuse permitted access; access controls need separation of duties and oversight.
38|21|RETAIN|DIRECT|Authenticated fresh challenges or checked one-time nonces prevent acceptance of captured device commands.|Device reset, storage limits and concurrent sessions must not reopen the replay window.
38|63|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Authenticated timestamps restrict the age of accepted commands with a defined time window.|Repeated messages inside the window remain possible without additional replay tracking; clock rollback must be handled.
39|64|QUALIFY|DETECTION|User behavior analytics can flag unusual access or exports for investigation.|Intent cannot be inferred reliably from anomaly alone; privacy and legitimate role changes require context.
39|65|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Least privilege limits the resources and actions an insider or stolen account can abuse.|Required legitimate access can still be misused, so this is impact reduction rather than complete prevention.
40|66|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Non-executable data pages obstruct injection that depends on executing bytes in those pages.|Code reuse, executable mappings and other injection paths remain; DEP does not remove the vulnerability.
40|67|RETAIN|DIRECT|Memory-safe construction and safe process interfaces can remove the defect enabling a specific injection path.|The actual path must be identified; a general secure-coding label cannot certify every process boundary.
41|68|RETAIN|DIRECT|Consistent locking protects a correctly scoped shared-state invariant from unsafe concurrent interleaving.|All participants must use the lock, and deadlocks or cross-process resource changes require design.
41|69|RETAIN|DIRECT|A suitable atomic operation or transaction keeps the complete check-and-update invariant indivisible.|Atomic individual reads are insufficient when the security decision spans multiple operations.
42|70|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Anti-malware detection can find and quarantine supported software keyloggers.|Hardware loggers and privileged or novel software require different inspection and recovery steps.
42|71|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|An on-screen keyboard may bypass a logger that observes only physical key events.|Screen capture, pointer capture and browser hooks can still collect the input; do not present it as endpoint security.
43|72|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Appropriate Secure, HttpOnly and SameSite cookie attributes reduce distinct token exposure paths.|No attribute makes a stolen live token universally unusable; XSS and endpoint compromise remain relevant.
43|73|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Server-enforced idle and absolute lifetime limits reduce the usable window of a stolen session.|An attacker can act before expiration or keep some sessions active; invalidation and reauthentication need additional design.
44|74|RETAIN|DIRECT|Independent source checking interrupts action based on a fabricated security instruction.|Evidence can be incomplete; verification must include date, applicability and the actual requested action.
44|75|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|An exercised response plan gives staff a safe route to triage questionable incident claims.|A plan alone does not establish that the claim is true or block harmful instructions.
45|76|RETAIN|DIRECT|Bound query parameters keep untrusted values from changing SQL syntax.|Dynamic identifiers and unsafe query paths still need controlled selection and review.
45|77|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A WAF can block recognized SQL injection patterns while code remediation is arranged.|Bypasses and false positives remain; it is not a replacement for separating SQL instructions and data.
46|78|CORRECT|DIRECT|Interpret Tokens specifically as server-validated request-bound anti-CSRF values in this mapping.|Generic session or bearer tokens are not automatically anti-CSRF protection; XSS or leaked tokens can defeat it.
46|79|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Appropriate SameSite policy reduces cookie sending in selected cross-site contexts.|It is not sufficient for every CSRF situation; unsafe endpoints and same-site attacks remain concerns.
47|80|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Lockouts or progressive restrictions slow online guesses on the protected authentication path.|Rigid lockouts can be abused for denial of service and do not stop offline cracking or all distributed spraying.
47|81|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Automation challenges can add friction to some online guessing tools.|Solvers, relays and bypasses exist; CAPTCHA alone is not a sufficient authentication or guessing defense.
48|82|CORRECT|PARTIAL|Prefer long unique passwords and common-value blocklists over the legacy idea of predictable complexity recipes.|Composition rules alone can produce guessable patterns; phishing and credential theft are different attack paths.
48|83|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Unique salts prevent shared offline precomputation against stored verifiers.|Salts do not block online dictionary guesses or eliminate offline guessing of an individual weak password.
49|84|RETAIN|DETECTION|Correlation across accounts and time can identify low-rate password spraying missed by per-account thresholds.|An alert needs response and sufficient telemetry; distributed slow attempts can remain difficult to distinguish.
49|85|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Appropriate additional authentication factors reduce takeover with a guessed password alone.|Phishable factors, weak recovery and already stolen sessions can undermine protection; prefer phishing-resistant methods.
50|86|RETAIN|DIRECT|Per-record random salts make one reusable rainbow-table computation impractical across a user database.|Use an appropriate password KDF too; salts do not create entropy in a weak password.
50|87|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Cost-tuned password derivation increases the cost of computing candidates or tables.|Cost alone does not diversify records; unique salts are needed to prevent shared precomputation.
51|88|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Confirmed exposure reports can trigger credential invalidation and investigation before further stuffing attempts.|Breach intelligence is incomplete and delayed; observing exposure does not itself prevent a login.
51|89|RETAIN|DIRECT|Different passwords for different services break the cross-service reuse prerequisite of credential stuffing.|A credential stolen from the target service or a compromised password manager remains exposed.
52|90|CORRECT|DIRECT|Require removal of shared factory defaults during secure provisioning or after confirmed exposure.|Do not reinterpret this as arbitrary periodic password changes without evidence; predictable replacement patterns can remain weak.
52|91|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Hardening removes unused default accounts and restricts reachable management interfaces.|A baseline must actually check credentials and effective access; generic hardening does not prove every default is gone.
53|92|RETAIN|DIRECT|Removing deprecated versions and weak fallback choices prevents negotiation of those specific options.|Remaining implementations can still have flaws; compatibility and authenticated negotiation need testing.
53|93|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|HSTS reduces browser HTTP fallback for the protected origin after valid policy learning or appropriate preload.|It does not prevent every TLS protocol downgrade and does not authenticate an unrelated look-alike domain.
54|94|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Wireless sensors and inventory comparison can find unapproved access points and support assessed response.|Detection can miss radios or misclassify legitimate equipment; automated disruption needs legal and operational review.
54|95|REMOVE_PROPOSED|PARTIAL|MAC lists can assist limited local device administration.|Spoofable MAC addresses do not authenticate an access point or reliably prevent unauthorized AP deployment; do not teach this as rogue-AP prevention.
55|96|RETAIN|PARTIAL|A rated installed lock with controlled keys increases resistance to specified manipulation techniques.|Weak frames, alternate entrances and stolen authorized keys can bypass the cylinder.
55|97|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Electronic access monitoring can detect an unexpected opening or add independent entry verification.|An electronic label does not imply mechanical pick resistance and an alert may occur after entry.
56|98|CORRECT|DIRECT|Use cryptographic tag authentication with protected keys and fresh challenges, rather than static identifier acceptance.|Encrypted stored data alone does not prevent cloning or relay; reader and protocol design must be assessed.
56|99|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Appropriate shielding limits opportunities for unauthorized radio reading while the credential is enclosed.|It does not protect a credential during legitimate use or invalidate a copy already obtained.
57|39|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Validate function events and inputs before they influence resource selection or execution.|Valid input can still invoke an overprivileged function, and dependency flaws require additional controls.
57|100|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Scoped service identities and resource policies reduce the reachable data and actions of a compromised function.|IAM does not repair vulnerable code and overbroad trust relationships can defeat intended boundaries.
58|101|RETAIN|DIRECT|Authenticated sequence values and receiver state reject already accepted protocol messages.|Reset, wraparound and session binding must not reopen acceptance of captured values.
58|102|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A genuinely one-time code can prevent reuse of a particular authentication value when it is bound and checked correctly.|It does not provide replay protection for arbitrary application commands or prevent live relay before use.
59|85|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Strong MFA can reduce takeover from a phished password alone.|Some factors can be phished, and phishing can pursue payments or malware rather than authentication.
59|103|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Mail inspection can quarantine supported malicious links, attachments and sender patterns before delivery.|New lures and compromised legitimate senders can pass; false positives require managed handling.
59|104|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Relevant training improves recognition, verification and reporting of phishing requests.|It is a complementary human layer, not proof that every attack will be recognized.
60|105|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Independent verification of identity and intent can interrupt a tailored request before sensitive action.|Familiar context and a genuine compromised mailbox can still mislead; domain authentication is not enough.
60|106|CORRECT|PARTIAL|Specify the actual mail or endpoint capability used to inspect the spear-phishing delivery path.|Threat Protection is too broad to imply a standardized or universally effective defense without a stated mechanism.
60|107|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Authorized simulations rehearse response to tailored lures and expose workflow weaknesses.|An exercise does not verify a real sender or block an actual payload.
61|108|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Role-specific practice helps executives and assistants recognize authority and confidentiality pretexts.|Senior staff still need enforceable verification procedures and technical controls; training alone is insufficient.
61|109|RETAIN|DIRECT|An independently verified request and dual approval can interrupt high-value impersonation before a transfer or disclosure.|The second channel must not be supplied by or controlled through the suspicious communication.
61|110|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Limiting sensitive-data access reduces what a compromised executive workflow can expose.|It does not authenticate the incoming request and cannot prevent misuse of legitimately permitted data.
62|111|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Independent navigation to a known service avoids trusting an unexpected SMS destination.|The trusted device or service can still be compromised; users need an accessible verification route.
62|112|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Supported mobile protections can flag some malicious destinations, apps or unsafe device conditions.|They cannot establish the truth of every text request and platform sandboxing limits visibility.
62|113|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Text filtering reduces delivery of recognized spam or malicious-message patterns.|Spoofed senders and novel wording can bypass filtering; a delivered message is not verified as safe.
63|41|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Check changed payment or disclosure instructions through established business evidence and contacts.|A generic instruction to verify must be turned into a defined independent process.
63|114|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Enforced DMARC reduces certain unauthorized uses of a protected mail domain.|It does not stop fraud through a compromised legitimate mailbox or a look-alike domain and does not validate invoice intent.
63|115|RETAIN|DIRECT|Independent financial approvals and payee-change checks constrain the transaction even if email is deceptive.|Collusion, unsafe exceptions and compromised approvers can still undermine the workflow.
64|116|RETAIN|DIRECT|Examine primary evidence, context and independent corroboration before treating a claim as true.|Evidence may remain uncertain; false information alone does not establish intentional deception.
64|117|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Moderation can reduce circulation of material violating an organization's stated platform policy.|Moderation is not a universal truth test and can remove legitimate speech if applied poorly.
64|118|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Instruction can teach source assessment, uncertainty and responsible sharing.|Training does not directly correct a false claim or establish the facts of a particular event.
65|119|RETAIN|DIRECT|Risk-appropriate identity verification can reject a false claimant before sensitive enrollment or recovery.|Verified identity does not automatically authorize the requested action; evidence and fallback quality matter.
65|118|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Training helps staff challenge claimed authority and use approved verification routes.|Awareness alone does not authenticate a person or enforce resource permissions.
65|120|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Role-scoped permissions limit what an impersonated or compromised identity can access.|RBAC does not prove the identity is genuine; broad or stale role membership can still enable misuse.
66|121|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Blocking a known compromised destination reduces visits while remediation and investigation proceed.|Previously unclassified or selectively served content can evade filters, and legitimate workflow disruption must be managed.
66|122|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Endpoint telemetry can reveal exploitation following a visit to a trusted but compromised site.|Detection may occur after execution and depends on sensor visibility and timely response.
66|123|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Relevant web or endpoint scanning can identify recognized malicious content or exposed vulnerabilities.|A clean scan is point-in-time evidence, not proof that selectively served or novel content is safe.
67|38|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Education encourages trusted navigation and careful checking of domain names.|Users cannot reliably detect every visual similarity or encoded hostname variation.
67|124|RETAIN|DETECTION|Registration and domain monitoring can discover look-alike infrastructure for investigation and response.|Similarity does not prove malice and discovery alone does not block an early victim visit.
67|125|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Browser look-alike warnings add a prompt to verify an unusual destination.|Heuristics can miss new patterns or be ignored; they are not authoritative domain-ownership verification.
68|123|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Controlled scanning can detect supported malicious files before an approved media transfer.|Unknown malware and USB devices emulating keyboards may not be detected by file scanning.
68|126|RETAIN|DIRECT|Endpoint device policy can block unapproved removable storage or other supported USB classes.|Coverage must match the device class; storage-only rules do not necessarily block malicious peripheral emulation.
68|127|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|A workable media policy defines approved devices, inspection and reporting routes.|Written instructions alone do not stop a connected device; technical enforcement and usable alternatives matter.
69|128|RETAIN|DIRECT|A safe access-control vestibule can require individual passage and verification at the boundary.|Emergency egress, accessibility and actual occupancy detection must be assessed.
69|129|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Individual badge checks support controlled entry and revocation.|A reader at one door does not necessarily stop someone following a badge holder through it.
69|130|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Trained personnel can observe entry and safely challenge unverified accompaniment.|Coverage, fatigue, social pressure and emergency conditions can limit effectiveness.
70|131|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Antivirus can recognize and quarantine supported infected files and execution behavior.|Novel or concealed infection can be missed and prior compromise may require more than quarantine.
70|132|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Applicable updates remove known vulnerabilities that some infected content exploits.|Viruses can also run through user authorization; updates do not guarantee removal of an existing infection.
70|133|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Safer navigation and download handling reduce some exposure to infected programs.|Legitimate sites and publishers can be compromised; user caution is not a complete malware boundary.
71|134|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Service-access rules reduce reachable propagation paths used by a worm.|Allowed vulnerable services and internal paths may still permit spread; firewall policy does not patch hosts.
71|135|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Enforced zone boundaries limit lateral reach between network segments.|Permitted paths and shared administration can bridge zones; segmentation does not eradicate infected systems.
71|136|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|IPS signatures or protocol checks can block recognized worm exploit traffic.|Novel exploits, encryption and evasion reduce coverage; false positives can disrupt legitimate service.
72|38|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Education helps users avoid deceptive installers and report suspicious software offers.|A convincing or trusted-source Trojan can still bypass human judgment.
72|137|RETAIN|DIRECT|An assessed execution allowlist can prevent unapproved disguised programs from running.|Approved signed tools or interpreters can be abused and approval rules require maintenance.
72|138|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Behavioral detections can reveal a Trojan's actions even when its file appears plausible.|False positives and evasion remain, and detection needs containment and recovery follow-through.
73|29|RETAIN|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Tested isolated or immutable backups enable data recovery without depending on the extortionist.|Backups do not prevent encryption, undo data theft or guarantee that restored systems are free of persistence.
73|139|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A specified mail or web gateway can reduce ransomware delivery through its inspected channel.|The broad gateway label is not a universal barrier; direct access, stolen credentials and unknown payloads can bypass it.
73|140|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Applicable patching removes known exploitation paths used by some ransomware intrusions.|Credential abuse and social engineering can remain, and patching does not reverse existing compromise.
74|85|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|MFA can reduce an attacker's ability to obtain remote access with a stolen password alone.|It does not stop a Trojan already running or an attacker using an existing victim session or outbound tunnel.
74|141|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Restrict unnecessary remote-control service exposure and assess outbound connection policy.|A Trojan may use permitted protocols and destinations, so closing inbound ports is not sufficient.
74|142|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Unusual process or connection behavior can indicate covert remote control for investigation.|Legitimate remote administration may look unusual, and attackers can blend into permitted activity.
75|37|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Correlated endpoint and network observations can identify devices contacting botnet infrastructure.|Indicators can be stale and monitoring alone does not remove the bot or block every control route.
75|143|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Isolating affected devices limits their ability to receive commands or attack other resources.|Isolation does not eradicate malware and must preserve safe response and operational requirements.
75|144|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Authorized sinkholing can redirect supported command channels and identify infected clients.|Alternative channels and embedded addresses may survive; authority and collateral effects must be assessed.
76|145|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Sufficiently provisioned upstream scrubbing filters flood traffic before the victim's constrained link.|Capacity, routing and application-layer filtering limits remain; there is no unlimited mitigation guarantee.
76|146|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Traffic measurements help distinguish attack patterns and select an appropriate mitigation.|Analysis does not itself block traffic or restore exhausted network capacity.
76|147|RETAIN|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|An established ISP response route enables filtering or routing changes outside the customer's limited edge.|Provider capability, contract and response time vary; coordination must be prepared before an incident.
77|148|RETAIN|DIRECT|Correctly validated TLS protects the intended transport session against intermediary observation and alteration.|Misissued trust, user overrides, compromised endpoints and application-level identity errors remain concerns.
77|149|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Well-managed pins can narrow accepted service identities in controlled clients.|Rollover errors can cause outages, and pinning does not make a compromised endpoint trustworthy.
77|150|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A verified VPN protects traffic across the segment between its authenticated endpoints.|It does not authenticate every destination after tunnel termination or make a deceptive Wi-Fi enrollment page safe.
78|151|RETAIN|DIRECT|Context-correct encoding or safe text APIs prevent untrusted plain text becoming executable browser syntax.|The encoder must match the output context; unsafe script or URL interpolation may need redesign instead.
78|152|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A restrictive correctly deployed CSP can reduce selected script-execution paths as defense in depth.|Permissive policy or trusted-script abuse can bypass protection; unsafe output handling still needs repair.
78|153|RETAIN|DIRECT|A maintained HTML sanitizer can enforce an intentionally allowed rich-markup subset.|Configuration, parser differences and post-sanitization mutation can reintroduce unsafe behavior.
79|154|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Resolve paths using platform-aware rules and verify confinement through safe APIs and appropriate authorization.|Naive prefix tests, symbolic links and check/use races can defeat incomplete normalization-based designs.
79|155|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A correctly configured reduced-privilege chroot confines ordinary path resolution to a chosen tree.|It is not a complete sandbox; privileged operations and unsafe descriptors can undermine it.
80|67|RETAIN|DIRECT|Bounds-safe interfaces and memory-safe construction remove the defective write rather than just complicating exploitation.|Unsafe interoperability and unchecked paths still need review and boundary testing.
80|156|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|ASLR makes exploit addresses less predictable where sufficient randomization is available.|Information leaks, low entropy and code reuse can reduce benefit; the overflow still exists.
80|157|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Compiler canaries detect some protected stack overwrites before control returns.|Not all writes or objects are covered, and leaked guard values can be bypassed.
81|33|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Permission and configuration reviews can identify escalation paths and verify remediation.|A periodic audit is not continuous enforcement or a patch for an unknown kernel vulnerability.
81|65|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Reducing standing privileges and controlling elevation limits attack opportunity and impact.|Required privileges can still be abused; escalation defects in trusted components require direct remediation.
82|158|RETAIN|DIRECT|Assessed restriction or retirement of NTLM removes the specific accepted path for NTLM-hash authentication abuse.|Compatibility and fallback need verification; other stolen credentials or Kerberos material remain risks.
82|159|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A correctly planned Kerberos migration can reduce reliance on NTLM when fallback is actually restricted.|Kerberos alone is not a complete pass-the-hash defense and does not prevent stolen-key or stolen-ticket attacks.
83|160|CORRECT|PARTIAL|HMAC supplies shared-key message authentication for a protocol designed to use it.|It is not a generic replacement for a collision-sensitive public signature or unkeyed integrity design; do not claim universal collision prevention.
83|161|RETAIN|DIRECT|Replacing a broken primitive with an appropriate reviewed construction addresses its known collision-security deficiency.|A new name or longer digest alone does not establish the complete protocol's security.
84|34|RETAIN|DIRECT|Appropriate authenticated encryption protects payload contents from unauthorized passive observers on the covered path.|Metadata may remain visible and compromised endpoints see plaintext; capture itself may be authorized and legitimate.
84|162|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Correctly enforced VLAN boundaries reduce some local access to unrelated traffic.|VLANs do not encrypt packets or replace authorization, and permitted routing or misconfiguration can expose traffic.
85|163|QUALIFY|DIRECT|AES in an appropriate reviewed construction is designed to tolerate known plaintext rather than relying on secret message formats.|The mode, nonce and key use still matter; AES alone is not a complete encryption system.
85|164|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Protected keys and appropriate lifecycles prevent weaknesses outside the cipher from exposing encrypted data.|Key management does not repair a cryptanalytic flaw in the chosen construction.
86|165|RETAIN|DIRECT|Established reviewed constructions avoid dependence on unassessed proprietary ciphertext-obscurity assumptions.|Correct implementation, randomness and key use still require verification; metadata leakage is a separate property.
86|166|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Appropriate key lifecycle controls can limit exposure after compromise or within a designed cryptoperiod.|Rotation does not repair a broken cipher and arbitrary periodic password changes are not a universal defense.
87|167|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Validating the expected enterprise authentication-server identity against appropriate CA trust resists a false network server.|A certificate from any trusted issuer is not sufficient without the expected identity; user overrides can defeat validation.
87|168|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Wireless observations and inventory checks can identify infrastructure imitating a trusted network.|Detection can occur after association and does not itself guarantee credential protection.
88|169|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Enforced Protected Management Frames authenticate specified robust management traffic against forgery.|They do not cover all wireless frames or prevent radio jamming; negotiation and compatibility need checking.
88|170|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Wireless intrusion sensors can alert on suspicious disconnect patterns for investigation.|An IDS does not automatically block frames, and interference or legitimate events can resemble an attack.
89|150|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|A trusted VPN can protect subsequent traffic over an untrusted network segment.|It does not prevent unsafe automatic association, authenticate the AP or protect credentials typed into a fake pre-tunnel page.
89|171|RETAIN|DIRECT|Disabling unsafe automatic association and removing obsolete profiles reduces opportunistic response-based network impersonation.|A user can still manually join a false network; strong configured network authentication remains important.
90|172|RETAIN|PARTIAL|An appropriately installed door assembly increases resistance to specified forced-entry attempts.|Locks, frames, anchoring and alternate routes matter; life-safety and accessibility requirements must remain intact.
90|173|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Intrusion alarms can notify responders when a physical barrier is forced or opened unexpectedly.|Detection may occur after entry and needs timely response; it does not strengthen the barrier itself.
91|174|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Enforcing appropriate SELinux policy restricts resources a container process can access beyond ordinary permissions.|Permissive mode, broad policy and kernel vulnerabilities can undermine confinement.
91|175|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Namespaces reduce shared resource views as one component of container isolation.|They are not a complete security boundary; host sockets, privileges and kernel defects can permit breakout.
92|132|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Applicable verified updates remove known device vulnerabilities used for bot recruitment.|Default credentials, unsupported devices and persistence after compromise may still need separate treatment.
92|135|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Enforced network zones limit access to and movement from compromised IoT fleets.|Segmentation does not remove bots and cannot automatically absorb traffic aimed at an external victim.
93|176|QUALIFY|PARTIAL|Strong verified network isolation removes direct remote paths where the operational design permits it.|Media, maintenance laptops and supply-chain routes can cross the boundary; a claimed air gap is not immunity.
93|177|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Industrial boundary policy restricts approved control-system paths and supported protocol operations.|Unsafe filtering can cause physical-process disruption; safety engineering and operational validation are necessary.
94|178|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Supplier and build-practice assessments identify risks and enforceable requirements before reliance on a dependency.|A favorable assessment cannot guarantee future publisher or build integrity.
94|179|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Trusted signature verification detects tampering after signing and binds a release to an accepted publisher key.|A compromised build or signing key can produce a validly signed malicious release; signatures do not prove benign code.
95|120|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Appropriately scoped cluster API roles restrict who can create or change powerful resources.|RBAC does not inspect every unsafe pod setting and overbroad role grants can permit escalation.
95|180|CORRECT|DIRECT|Use supported workload admission controls such as Pod Security Admission and assessed admission policies.|The removed PodSecurityPolicy API is not a current deployment recommendation; runtime and kernel risks remain.
96|181|QUALIFY|DETECTION|Heuristic or behavior-based detection can identify exploit-like actions before a precise signature exists.|False positives and unseen techniques remain; do not claim that heuristics detect every zero-day.
96|182|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Assessed intelligence guides hunting and protective priorities as new exploit evidence emerges.|Intelligence can be incomplete or stale and does not itself block an unknown vulnerability.
97|37|QUALIFY|DETECTION|DNS query, response and administration observations can reveal selected anomalous behavior or unauthorized changes.|The broad DNS attack category needs a concrete threat; monitoring is not prevention of all DNS failures.
97|183|CORRECT|DIRECT|Specify DNSSEC validation, encrypted transport, registrar security or resilience according to the actual threat scenario.|Secure DNS is not one universal control; encryption and data authentication solve different problems.
98|143|RETAIN|PARTIAL|Isolating a compromised browser or endpoint and invalidating sessions reduces further transaction exposure during response.|Containment does not eradicate the implant; sensitive work must move to a trusted environment.
98|184|CORRECT|PARTIAL|Review and remove unapproved or excessive-permission extensions as a specific browser-hardening measure.|Adding an arbitrary extension is not protection against a compromised browser, and extensions may be the attack source.
99|34|CORRECT|PARTIAL|Authenticated wireless encryption protects content against unauthorized reading even when network surveying is possible.|It does not prevent detecting or mapping radio networks; authorized wardriving is not inherently malicious.
99|185|REMOVE_PROPOSED|PARTIAL|Suppressing some SSID advertisements only changes simple discovery presentation.|Management exchanges still reveal network identity; hiding SSID is not meaningful protection from surveying or unauthorized access.
100|41|QUALIFY|INDIRECT_SUPPORT|Independent release and publisher verification supports a trustworthy update decision.|A generic verification instruction needs concrete evidence and cannot certify that an authentic publisher was not compromised.
100|179|QUALIFY|DIRECT|Signature verification with an independently trusted key detects package tampering after signing.|A correctly signed update can still be malicious if the publisher or signing pipeline was compromised.
